      "level": 1,
      "content": "Why Dynamic Memory Allocation?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Before we learn about dynamic memory allocation, let's first look at some limitations of an array."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "content": "int marks[10];"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here, the size of the array is 10, which is fixed and cannot be changed."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Creating an array of fixed size can lead to two issues:"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "If we only need to store the marks of, say, 3 students, then we have wasted memory.",
        "If we need to store the marks of more than 10 students, we cannot do that."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "To solve this issue, the concept of dynamic memory allocation was introduced in C++ programming."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Dynamic memory allocation allows us to allocate memory after we run our program (during run-time)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Now that we know why we need dynamic memory allocation, let's learn about it in further detail."
    },
